# Test Structure and Organization Rules

Single `indexed` package (`src/indexed/`) — tests mirror it under `tests/unit/indexed/`.
There is no separate `tests/integration/` directory; unit vs. integration vs. other
test kinds are distinguished by **pytest markers**, not by directory, except for
`tests/system/` and `tests/characterization/`, which are their own top-level dirs.

## Directory Structure

```text
tests/
├── unit/                 # tests/unit/indexed/{package}/{module}/test_{component}.py
│                          # mirrors src/indexed/ — most tests live here, marker-classified
├── system/                # CLI-driven, multi-module workflow tests (real components,
│                          # mocked external services only)
├── characterization/      # smoke tests against a real on-disk collection, built through
│                          # the real engine — regression-guard behavior, not unit isolation
├── benchmarks/             # performance tests (pytest-benchmark)
└── fixtures/               # shared test fixtures/data
```

## Test Type Definitions

### Unit tests (`tests/unit/`, `@pytest.mark.unit`)
**Scope:** a single class/function in isolation, all dependencies mocked, fast
(target < 100ms/test).

### Integration tests (`tests/unit/`, `@pytest.mark.integration`)
**Scope:** multiple real components exercised together (e.g. real FAISS index +
real embeddings), still discovered under `tests/unit/` but marked so they can be
excluded from a fast-only run (`pytest -m unit`) and skip gracefully when a
dependency they need (e.g. a cached embedding model) isn't available.

### System tests (`tests/system/`)
**Scope:** complete user-facing workflows spanning CLI → service → engine →
storage, real components throughout, mocking only external services (Jira/
Confluence APIs).

### Characterization tests (`tests/characterization/`)
**Scope:** smoke tests against a real, on-disk collection built through the real
engine — regression guards for end-to-end behavior, not isolated unit coverage.

## Test Markers

Registered in `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.pytest.ini_options] markers`) —
`--strict-markers` rejects anything else:
- `unit` — fast, fully mocked
- `unit_api` — unit tests exercising a mocked API surface
- `integration` — multiple real components, still under `tests/unit/`
- `api` — tests exercising a connector API surface
- `connectors` — connector functionality tests
- `indexed` — main app integration tests
- `benchmark` — performance tests
- `slow` — tests taking noticeably longer than the fast-test norm

**Convention:** a whole test FILE that's uniformly one kind gets a module-level
`pytestmark = pytest.mark.unit` (see `tests/unit/indexed/config/test_cli.py`).
A file that mixes fast unit tests with the occasional real-component test marks
just that one test/function directly (see
`tests/unit/indexed/core/test_engine_facade_v2.py`'s
`@pytest.mark.integration` on its migration-order test).

## Anti-Patterns

❌ Real FAISS/embeddings/network in a test with no `integration`/`api`/`slow`
   marker — it silently slows down `pytest -m unit` runs meant to stay fast.
❌ A brand-new marker not registered in `pyproject.toml` — fails
   `--strict-markers` immediately.
❌ Mocking so much of a `characterization/` or `system/` test that it stops
   exercising real behavior — that defeats their purpose as regression guards.
